                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "1fdfb11a-b186-4c3e-ace1-f14dbf6b54cf",
                        "source_entry_id": "42e30832-8a19-3789-91bb-93c55fd85452",
                        "source_sense_id": "1fceb5e7-5e0e-33d1-a2ed-dfecbf08363e",
                        "target_lang_word": "Guinea-Bissauan",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Guinea-Bissauan",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "da814e9f-f82e-3b58-aced-a47de3d23466",
                                        "entry_id": "e194d381-d3aa-3a27-82b9-c06e7c1112fe",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of or from Guinea-Bissau."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "a690c3ac-9bca-4106-8b66-bd7607300db2",
                                                "sense_id": "da814e9f-f82e-3b58-aced-a47de3d23466",
                                                "text": "Copious amounts of palm oil, tomatoes, chili, fish, and goat’s meat are considered traditional staples in Guinea-Bissauan cooking."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "85e96d82-089f-4ca4-8534-c8091133e365",
                                                "sense_id": "da814e9f-f82e-3b58-aced-a47de3d23466",
                                                "text": "These events and the silences and taboos surrounding them helped create the conditions for the longevity of the ‘linha de Cabral’ or ‘Cabral’s programme’ as a key reference of the erstwhile liberation movement and Guinea-Bissauan politics in general until the late 1990s."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f72b3bec-4bfe-4b16-9102-2371e135c72c",
                                                "sense_id": "da814e9f-f82e-3b58-aced-a47de3d23466",
                                                "text": "Cabral and Pereira looked to unify both nations until Cabral’s prime minister, the former commander of the Guinea-Bissauan armed forces, João Bernardo Vieira, led a military coup against Cabral in 1980 on the grounds that Cabral’s Cape Verdean origins (like his brother, Amílcar, he was born in Bissau to Cape Verdean mixed-race parents) compromised his dedication to the Guinea-Bissauan people."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            },
                            {
                                "target_word": "Guinea-Bissauan",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "96dce12c-9c6c-394a-8f75-22c5a211b1a1",
                                        "entry_id": "8f16582b-3f70-3f0a-a21a-afa14d583d07",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A person from Guinea-Bissau or of Guinea-Bissaun descent."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "744afac8-d65f-4906-8562-ca2d5e370ab6",
                                                "sense_id": "96dce12c-9c6c-394a-8f75-22c5a211b1a1",
                                                "text": "A noticeable proportion of Guinea-Bissauans are mixed race Crioulo-speakers; mostly Cape Verdean, but also descendants of the small number of settlers and traders who came direct from Portugal."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4551d9ca-8782-43e7-b411-692baf0fc1e8",
                                                "sense_id": "96dce12c-9c6c-394a-8f75-22c5a211b1a1",
                                                "text": "Hoyt said, ‘When you look at me, I don’t want you to see a Guinea-Bissauan. My passport is a detail. It helped me through a personal crisis. Do me the courtesy of seeing me as I really am, a colleague and a fellow human being.’"
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "771496a4-91a9-440e-a1d9-41c59458bd6a",
                                                "sense_id": "96dce12c-9c6c-394a-8f75-22c5a211b1a1",
                                                "text": "In other words, the cocaine trade may be seen to provide a possibility for migration when in Bissau, just as selling cocaine may provide one of the few ways that Guinea-Bissauans can gain an income once they arrive in Europe, yet in doing so it makes both migration from Bissau and life in Europe increasingly difficult, as Guinea-Bissauans are increasingly policed and met with suspicion."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
